/** @page tarformatsguide TAR Formats

    @tableofcontents

    The module supports multiple TAR formats:

    |!Constant|!Description
    |@ref Qore::Tar::TAR_FORMAT_USTAR|POSIX.1-1988 ustar format (100-char filename limit)
    |@ref Qore::Tar::TAR_FORMAT_PAX|POSIX.1-2001 pax format (recommended, no practical limits)
    |@ref Qore::Tar::TAR_FORMAT_GNU|GNU tar format (extended attributes support)
    |@ref Qore::Tar::TAR_FORMAT_V7|Original V7 Unix tar format (legacy)

    The PAX format is recommended for new archives as it supports:
    - Unlimited filename length
    - Unlimited file size
    - Unicode filenames
    - Extended attributes
    - Nanosecond timestamps

    @section tarformatsnames Entry Names

    Entry names and link targets are UTF-8 strings: names passed to the module in another encoding are
    converted to UTF-8, and names read from an archive are returned as UTF-8 strings.  This does not depend on
    the process locale: the module runs libarchive under a UTF-8 character type locale on the calling thread
    for the duration of each call, so non-ASCII names such as \c 日本語.txt are written, read, looked up and
    extracted exactly even in the \c "C" locale.  The process locale is never changed, and callbacks, such as
    an extraction progress callback, run under the thread's own locale.  A host without any UTF-8 locale
    keeps the locale-dependent behavior.

    @subsection tarformatsnamesnfc Unicode Normalization of Names

    The same text can be written in Unicode with different code points: \c ř is one precomposed character in
    normalization form C (NFC), and \c r followed by a combining caron in normalization form D (NFD).  libarchive
    normalizes the UTF-8 names of pax headers when it reads them, to NFD on macOS and to NFC on other platforms,
    so a name cannot be read back exactly as it is stored.  The module therefore presents names in NFC on every
    platform:
    - entry names (@ref Qore::Tar::TarEntryInfo "TarEntryInfo" \c name, the names returned by extraction, and
      the files that extraction creates), link targets (\c link_target, and the targets of extracted links) and
      owner names (\c uname, \c gname) are in NFC, in archives of any format; an NFD name in a ustar or GNU
      header, which libarchive reads as it is stored, is presented in NFC as well
    - a name passed to a lookup (@ref Qore::Tar::TarFile::hasEntry() "hasEntry()",
      @ref Qore::Tar::TarFile::getEntry() "getEntry()", @ref Qore::Tar::TarFile::read() "read()",
      @ref Qore::Tar::TarFile::readText() "readText()", @ref Qore::Tar::TarFile::getInputStream() "getInputStream()",
      @ref Qore::Tar::TarFile::extract() "extract()", @ref Qore::Tar::TarFile::extractTo() "extractTo()") and the
      \c include and \c exclude patterns of extraction are compared in NFC, so a name can be given in either form
    - names are written exactly as they are given; entries copied when an archive is appended to are written with
      their names in NFC, as they are presented

    ASCII names are the same in every normalization form and are not affected.
*/
